<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emperor Xiaojing of Eastern Wei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">524–<a href="/years/552">552</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Yuan Shanjian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emperor Xiaojing of Eastern Wei, born Yuan Shanjian, was the only emperor of the Eastern Wei. The general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> placed him on the throne as a boy in 534 and moved the court to Ye. Real power lay with Gao Huan and his sons, and in 550 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wenxuan-of-northern-qi">Gao Yang</a> forced him to abdicate and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-qi">Northern Qi</a>. He was poisoned in 552.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yuan Shanjian was born in 524, the eldest son and heir of Yuan Dan, Prince of Qinghe, a grandson of Emperor Xiaowen. In 534 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-xiaowu-of-northern-wei">Emperor Xiaowu of Northern Wei</a>, a cousin of Yuan Dan, allied with the generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuwen-tai">Yuwen Tai</a> and Heba Sheng against the paramount general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> and fled to Yuwen's territory. Gao Huan entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> and, judging the arrogant Yuan Dan unfit, chose his son instead, saying a cousin should not succeed a cousin. Northern Wei was thus divided into Eastern and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a>, judging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a> too close to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-wei">Western Wei</a> and Liang, moved the capital to Yecheng and governed from his headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jinyang">Jinyang</a>, always treating the emperor with public respect. Yuan Dan died in 536, assassinated by Gao according to some historians. The young emperor was described as handsome and strong, able to leap a fence holding a stone lion, and skilled in riding, archery and literature. In 539 he married Gao Huan's second daughter.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-huan">Gao Huan</a> died in 547, his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-cheng">Gao Cheng</a> had the emperor closely watched by Cui Jishu. At a feast Gao Cheng insulted him and had Cui punch him three times. The emperor then plotted with his teacher Xun Ji and several princes, digging a tunnel out of the palace, but it was discovered; Gao Cheng put him under house arrest and executed leading conspirators. When Gao Cheng was assassinated in 549, the emperor remarked that power should return to the imperial clan, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wenxuan-of-northern-qi">Gao Yang</a> marched 8,000 guards into the palace and took control. In 550 he received the nine bestowments and then the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wenxuan-of-northern-qi">Gao Yang</a>, now Emperor Wenxuan of Northern Qi, created the former emperor Prince of Zhongshan on 10 June 550 and excused him from calling himself a subject. His wife, the new emperor's sister, now Princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiyuan">Taiyuan</a>, tasted his food to guard against poison. Around the new year 552, while she was at a palace feast, assassins forced him to drink poisoned wine, and his three sons were also killed. Emperor Wenxuan gave him the posthumous name Xiaojing and an imperial burial, but later, for reasons unknown, opened the tomb and threw the casket into the Zhang River.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emperor Xiaojing of Eastern Wei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Northern Wei emperor Xiaowu fled from Gao Huan's control to Yuwen Tai at Chang'an in 534, Gao Huan installed the boy <b>Yuan Shanjian</b> as Emperor Xiaojing and moved the court from Luoyang to Ye.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-wei">Eastern Wei</a></li></ul></section>
